<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Li Te</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 303</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century">3rd century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Li Te was a Ba-Di leader who led refugees fleeing famine in the north-west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a> around 298, under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-jin-dynasty">Western Jin dynasty</a>. When Jin officials tried to force the refugees back, he rebelled in 301 and fought the provincial governor Luo Shang around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, but was killed in 303. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xiong">Li Xiong</a> took Chengdu the next year and founded the Cheng Han state, which later honoured Li Te as an emperor.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/li-te.jpg" alt="Li Te" width="320" height="465" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: see below · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harvard_drs_49802792_%E7%B9%A1%E5%83%8F%E4%B8%89%E5%9C%8B%E6%BC%94%E7%BE%A9%E7%BA%8C%E7%B7%A8_v.1_(%E6%9D%8E%E7%89%B9).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Li Te, courtesy name Xuanxiu, came of a family of Zong or Bandun people from Baxi commandery, around present-day Langzhong in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sichuan">Sichuan</a>. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cao-cao">Cao Cao</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanzhong">Hanzhong</a> at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a>, Li Te's grandfather Li Hu led his people north to submit to him, and the family settled in Lüeyang Commandery, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gansu">Gansu</a>, where they mingled with the local Di; hence the name Ba Di. Li Te was the second of five sons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-mu">Li Mu</a> and was known for martial skills such as mounted archery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Li Te and his followers left the north after the revolt of the Di chieftain Qi Wannian, which broke out in 296, brought famine and military occupation. In 300 the Inspector of Yizhou, Zhao Xin, rebelled against the Jin, and Li Te's brother Li Xiáng joined him; in 301 Zhao Xin, wary of his popularity, had him and many kinsmen killed. Li Te and Li Liu marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, burned Zhao Xin's camp at Shiting in a night raid and sacked the city, and Zhao Xin was killed by a subordinate while fleeing.</p><p>The Jin court then sent Luo Shang. The order that the refugees return north, and the refusal, urged by Luo's subordinate Xin Ran, to reward those who had fought Zhao Xin, turned the refugees against the authorities. When Xin Ran posted notices demanding the Li family's heads, Li Te altered them to say the other refugee families were also to die, and many joined him. He destroyed a surprise attack on his camp, took Guanghan, and in 302 received the surrender of Zitong and Baxi and proclaimed himself Governor of Yizhou.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In January 303 Li Te took the lesser city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a> by surprise, declared an amnesty and a new reign era, and agreed to talks with Luo Shang, ignoring the warnings of Li Liu and Shangguan Dun. Luo's subordinate Ren Rui feigned surrender, learned Li Te's plans and returned to Chengdu. With the help of the local fortified communities, Luo Shang made a surprise attack on Li Te's weakly held camp; after two days of fighting Li Te was killed with Li Fu and Li Yuan, and their heads were sent to the capital.</p><p>The rebels chose Li Liu as their leader, and after his death later in 303 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-xiong">Li Xiong</a> succeeded him. Li Xiong founded the state of Cheng in 304, the first of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sixteen-kingdoms">Sixteen Kingdoms</a>, and honoured his father as King Jing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chengdu">Chengdu</a>, and after declaring himself emperor in 306, as Emperor Jing.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Li Te” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
